  "articleBody": "Remodeling is exciting, but electrical work can be tricky if not handled correctly. Steering clear of these common mistakes can save you time, money, and stress: \n\n1. Assuming the rough-in layout can be changed later: Once those wires are run and inspected, moving things around is a major undertaking. How to avoid it: Finalize your electrical plans and layouts before rough-in begins.\n\n2. Overlooking ceiling-space and panel-clearance conflicts: Many systems compete for space in ceilings, and your electrical panel needs clear access. How to avoid it: Ensure your contractor coordinates electrical with HVAC and plumbing to avoid clashes and ensures proper panel clearance.\n\n3. Underestimating permit or inspection costs and delays: Electrical work often requires permits and inspections, which can take time and money. How to avoid it: Work with a professional who factors these into the schedule and budget upfront, like HH Remodeling LLC.\n\n4. Not considering future needs: Adding an electric car charger or more high-power appliances later can mean another service upgrade. How to avoid it: Discuss your future electrical needs with your electrician during planning.\n\n5. Choosing an unlicensed or uninsured electrician: This can lead to unsafe work and liability issues. How to avoid it: Always verify your electrician’s licensing and insurance before they start any work.",
